  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n(Tuesday - Tuesday Night)\n\nThe surface ridge is expected to move far enough east by Tuesday \nthat weak return flow will begin in its wake. The moderating trend \nin temperatures is therefore expected to continue as highs reach \ncloser to the 90 degree mark. Increasing mid/high level clouds will \nhelp dampen the warmup slightly during the day, but then aid in \nkeeping temperatures milder Tuesday night as lows only drop back \ninto the mid 60s to low 70s from east to west.\n\n\n(Wednesday - Sunday)\n\nA broad longwave trough is forecast to carve out across the northern \nPlains/southern Canada by midweek. Multiple impulses are expected to \nmove across the base of this trough, with each one inducing weak \ncyclogenesis along a slow-moving cold front. This boundary is \nforecast to move into portions of the area by Wednesday afternoon. \nSurface convergence along this boundary in addition to increasing \nlow-level moisture convergence and mid/upper level diffluence aloft \nis expected to yield showers and thunderstorms. The best chances (60-\n80%) through Wednesday night are across northeast Missouri and \nwest-central Illinois in close proximity to the expected location of \nthe surface cold front. There may be at least a low threat for a few \nstrong to severe thunderstorms in the afternoon/evening hours given \nfairly strong westerly flow aloft for early August. Joint \nprobabilities on the LREF for at least 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE and 30+ \nknots of deep-layer shear are 40-50% early Wednesday evening in \nnortheast Missouri/west central Illinois. Precipitable water values \napproach 1.8\" along the front (>90th percentile of climatology). The \nslow equatorward progress of the cold front Wednesday night into \nThursday in conjunction with a strengthening nocturnal low-level jet \n(850-hPa meridional/v component of wind >90th percentile) also \nsuggest at least the possibility of locally heavy rainfall/isolated \nflash flooding.\n\nThere is pretty good consensus that the front will move toward \nsoutheast Missouri/southwest Illinois, but may tend to stall out. \nDeterministic guidance has a weaker low-level jet Thursday night, \nbut increasing moisture convergence may help yield an uptick in \nshower and thunderstorm activity along/north of the front. \n\nThe location of the boundary Friday through the weekend becomes \ncloudier with time. Multiple possibilities exist. Some deterministic \nguidance pushes the front back north, which would likely yield \nwarmer, but dry conditions. Another scenario is that the front \nlingers across the area and help provide the focus for additional \nshowers and thunderstorms. The boundary also could be modulated \nfarther south due to antecedent rounds of convection. The bottom \nline is that forecast specifics, both in terms of temperatures and \nprecipitation chances, are uncertain right now.",
